# Human public docs

When writing or editing README, CHANGELOG, release notes, runbooks, or GitHub release bodies:

## Voice
- Write like a careful student or indie developer: clear, short, factual.
- Prefer plain sentences over marketing or buzzwords.
- Do not sound like a chatbot summarizing a conversation.

## Never include
- Chat residue: “as you asked”, “as discussed”, “implemented what you told”, “per your request”, “see chat”
- Process leftovers: Bugbot / agent review tables, “vX candidate”, “UI polish continues”, apologetic screenshot caveats
- AI filler: “Premium…”, “Inspired by X, Y, Z…”, “seamless”, “robust”, “comprehensive”, “leverages”
- Fake roadmap theater in public docs: Sprint checklists as marketing, “Phase 1” / “Critical” section titles unless they are real project labels already in use
- Claims about unreleased features still only on a local branch

## Do include
- What the app is, who it is for, how to run it
- Accurate latest release / live URL
- Changelog or release bodies that name user-visible changes (and real fixes)
- Known limitations that affect users
- Download links for APKs when publishing

## Shape
- README: short intro → what it does → setup → doc links. Avoid duplicating long architecture essays.
- CHANGELOG / GitHub releases: every shipped version, plain language, no “bumped version to…” noise.
- Release notes: changes + download (+ test focus if useful). Drop internal review transcripts and “see chat” pointers.

Match the tone of a clean student portfolio repo: useful to an outsider in under a minute.
